# Resolution

Fixed in commit `1c6e141` (`Gate reclaim retries on pending metadata`).

Phase 1 of the reclaim-queue hardening plan is now shipped:

- added a storage metadata helper to answer whether a specific
  `(bucket, key, generation_id)` still has any durable reclaim record
- changed `PayloadLease::drop` so the final lease release only re-enqueues
  reclaim work when that generation is actually pending reclaim
- preserved the intended retry path for reclaim records that were queued
  earlier but could not be reclaimed yet because a read lease was still active

This resolves the read-amplified denial of service described in this finding:
ordinary reads of non-pending generations no longer manufacture reclaim queue
entries on final lease drop.

Added regression coverage:

- storage: exact reclaim-existence predicate across simple, segmented, and
  multipart reclaim tables
- coordinator: no reclaim work is enqueued when a read-only lease drops with
  no reclaim record
- coordinator: a pending reclaim is re-enqueued correctly after an earlier
  lease-gated reclaim attempt

Follow-up status:

- the broader queue backpressure / bounded-memory work remains tracked as phase
  2 in `plans/reclaim-queue-hardening-plan.md`
- this report is resolved because the specific read-driven enqueue bug has been
  fixed
