# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
The bug is real and reachable via the HTTP read path, but the impact is availability-only and requires sustained volume across unique objects/generations. Auth/bucket ACLs and deployment exposure affect who can trigger it. This aligns with a medium DoS rather than high/critical impact.
## Likelihood
high - Requires many unique read targets or the ability to create new generations; feasible for authenticated users or public buckets, but not fully unauthenticated in all deployments. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - Unbounded memory growth can crash or stall the single-node service, reducing availability for all clients; no confidentiality or integrity impact is shown.
## Assumptions
- The argmin-s3 HTTP listener is reachable by an attacker (public internet or internal network), not only localhost.
- The attacker can perform many object reads across unique (bucket, key, generation) tuples via valid credentials or public-read buckets.
- No external rate limiting or reverse-proxy backpressure is in place that would cap request volume.
- Ability to issue S3 GET/READ requests against many unique object generations (auth credentials or public bucket)
- Sufficient object diversity or write ability to create unique generations
## Path
Client -> GET/READ -> ReadHandle(lease) -> PayloadLease::drop -> enqueue reclaim -> unbounded queue -> OOM/DoS
## Path evidence
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:75-99` - argmin-s3 binds a TCP listener and serves the HTTP frontend, establishing the network-exposed runtime entrypoint.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:20-63` - Listen address is configurable via ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR with default 127.0.0.1:9000, indicating possible network exposure.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:198-218` - PayloadLease::drop enqueues object payload reclaim when the lease count reaches zero.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:330-369` - ReadHandle creation acquires object payload leases on reads.
- `crates/storage/src/node.rs:84-139` - SharedStorageNode stores reclaim_queue as an in-memory VecDeque/HashSet without a size limit.
- `crates/storage/src/node.rs:236-248` - enqueue_object_payload_reclaim unconditionally pushes unique roots into the queue with no bounding.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:2212-2219` - A single reclaim worker thread drains the queue, limiting drain rate.
## Narrative
Payload leases for object reads enqueue reclaim work on drop, and the reclaim queue is an in-memory VecDeque/HashSet with no size limit. Read paths acquire leases on every object read, so many unique reads (or writes to create new generations) will enqueue unique roots faster than the single reclaim worker can drain them. This makes memory exhaustion plausible from an in-scope HTTP read surface when the service is reachable and the attacker has read access (including public buckets).
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication and bucket ACLs
- Configurable max_connections
## Blindspots
- No runtime PoC execution in this environment; conclusions are based on static analysis only.
- No deployment manifests available to confirm actual network exposure (public vs internal).
- Actual object/versioning configuration could affect how easily unique generations are created.
